   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to renting of housing through marketplace providers",
    "basePrintNo": "S5523",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5523-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1101, 1131 & 1132, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5523\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to renting of housing through\nmarketplace providers\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill will allow marketplace providers to collect sales and occupan-\ncy taxes on behalf of its clients.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subdivision (c) of section 1101 of the tax law is amended by\nadding two new paragraphs 9 and 10. This section defines \"facilitates\noccupancy\", \"marketplace seller of occupancy\" and \"marketplace provider\nof occupancy\". This section allows for marketplace providers to assume\nthe sales tax collection and remittance responsibilities.\n\nSection 2. Amends paragraph 6 of subdivision (c) of section 1101 of the\ntax law, as amended by section 2 of part AA of chapter 57 of the laws of\n2010. Explicitly excludes from the definition of rent any charges or\nfees paid to the marketplace seller of occupancy.\n\nSection 3. Amends subdivision 1 of section 1131 of the tax law, as\namended by chapter 576 of the laws of 2014. Includes marketplace provid-\ners of occupancy as eligible to collect tax.\n\nSection 4. Section 1132 of the tax law is amended by adding a new subdi-\nvision (1). Requires the marketplace provider of occupancy to comply and\nkeep records of this section. This further provides for regulations to\npromulgate this section. Lastly, this section provides exemptions that a\nmarketplace seller of occupancy may not be required to collect tax.\n\nSection 5. Effective Date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe sharing economy is rapidly expanding and engaging more New Yorkers\nthroughout the state. Resourceful residents are making the most of their\nhome by allowing short-term guests to use spare rooms in their home, or\nrenting out their home during their vacations and business trips. This\nhas been common practice for hundreds of years, but new technologies,\nmobile apps, and websites make the process easier than ever before.\n\nUnfortunately, many New York residents do not have the knowledge or the\nmeans to complete the complicated tax processes that come with renting\ntheir homes for occupancy for a fee. This legislation will allow the\n\"facilitators\" of these occupancies, the websites and apps that list the\noccupancy and facilitate the transaction between guests and hosts, to\ncollect the sales and occupancy tax on behalf of the hosts. This bill\n\nwill provide hosting platforms with the tools necessary to collect and\nremit taxes will ensure the smooth flow of revenues to the state and\nmake life easier for hosts and guests. Such tax collection by a market-\nplace provider of occupancy on behalf of the state will be voluntary\nunder this law.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
